		<title type="html"><![CDATA[RawKee Physics]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=2034#p2034"><![CDATA[
Thanks for the info.<br><br>As for RawKee, it exports to the X3D file format. There is an X3D physics component proposal put by Yumetech.<br><a href="http://www.xj3d.org/extensions/rigid_physics.html" class="postlink">http://www.xj3d.org/extensions/rigid_physics.html</a><br><br>I'm attempting to match up Maya's Rigid Body Physics implementaiton to this X3D specification proposal. If there are additional physics features that Collada plug-ins add to Maya, I'd like to be able to map as many of them to X3D export as I can.<br><br>I collaborate with an independent game company that is developing a Java3D-based game engine which uses X3D as its main content format and Maya as its main production tool. A long range goal is to develop a Maya-based level editor for their Cosmic game engine. Cosmic has its own custom physics engine written from the ground up in java.<br><br>See Cosmic Birdie:<br><a href="http://www.imilabs.com/CosmicBirdie/" class="postlink">http://www.imilabs.com/CosmicBirdie/</a><br><a href="http://cosmic-game-engine.blogspot.com/" class="postlink">http://cosmic-game-engine.blogspot.com/</a><br><br>A bit about RawKee: <a href="http://images.autodesk.com/adsk/files/NDSU_CaseStudy.pdf" class="postlink">http://images.autodesk.com/adsk/files/N ... eStudy.pdf</a><br><br>So right now I'm poking around a bit trying to learn more about physics implementations in Maya.<br><br>Any advice is appreciated.<br><br>Thanks,<br><br>Aaron<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=967">flickertail</a> — Wed Oct 04, 2006 7:39 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
